            <ArticleTitle>A case of paradoxical air embolism in the coronary artery through a patent foramen ovale demonstrated by coronary CT angiography.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>167-9</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.clinimag.2012.04.001</ELocationID>
            <ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">S0899-7071(12)00113-1</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>We present a case of paradoxical air embolism (PAE) in the distal right coronary artery identified by coronary CT angiography. The underlying cause of PAE in the distal right coronary artery was a patent foramen ovale because the typical CT findings of a patent foramen ovale (i.e., combination of findings of the presence of a slit-like contrast column in the interatrial septum and a contrast jet through the septum) were identified by coronary CT angiography.</AbstractText>
